$DIR/main.p:18:6: a function literal that captures variables cannot be started with go: wg lives in the starting frame, which may be gone before the puroutine runs (spec section 4.7); wg has type stdsync.WaitGroup, which must not be copied, so allocate it with new and pass the pointer: wg := new(stdsync.WaitGroup); go func(wg *stdsync.WaitGroup, results *[4]int, i int) {...}(wg, results, i)
    A function literal that uses variables of the function around it reaches
    them in that function's stack frame. A go statement may start it after that
    function has returned, when the frame is gone.
    Pass the values the literal needs as arguments: they are copied when the go
    statement runs. Do not pass a Mutex, a WaitGroup or another sync type this
    way, since the copy is a different one: pass a pointer to it.
    wg holds a value of type stdsync.WaitGroup. Passing it as an argument would
    copy it, and a copy is a separate value: calling Done or Unlock on the copy
    never reaches the original, so a Wait or Lock on the original may never
    return. Pass a pointer to one instead, allocated with new, or declare the
    variable at package level.
    Go moves captured variables to the heap. Plutonium never allocates behind
    the program's back, so captured variables stay in their frame (spec section
    4.5).
    Allocate it with new and pass the pointer:
        wg := new(stdsync.WaitGroup)
        go func(wg *stdsync.WaitGroup, results *[4]int, i int) {
            ...
        }(wg, results, i)
    (see "go starts a puroutine" in the Plutonium spec, section 4.7)
    (run "pluto explain E2590" for a longer explanation)

